---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why does email break more often than websites after DNS changes?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Websites need one record (A). Email needs four working together (MX, SPF, DKIM, DMARC): four times the chances to break."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Can I receive mail if MX records are missing?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Not to your domain: senders have no routing instruction. Some servers fall back to the A record, but this is unreliable."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "How long until email works after fixing MX?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The DNS fix propagates in minutes to hours. In-flight mail that bounced is lost (sender gets a bounce notice); new mail delivers normally."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Should I test email after DNS changes?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Yes: send to yourself at Gmail, Outlook, and a colleague. Check both delivery and spam placement."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What if only external senders can't reach me?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Internal mail may work via direct delivery. The issue is your MX records as seen by external resolvers: check propagation."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Does changing my A record affect email?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Only indirectly: if your mail server runs on the same IP as your website, changing the A record redirects web traffic but not mail (MX points at a hostname, not an IP)."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Email Stopped Working After a DNS Change | PromptDNS"
  description="email stopped working after DNS change"
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Email Stopped After DNS Change</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">Email Stopped Working After a DNS Change</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Email breaks after DNS changes more often than websites do, because mail uses more record types (MX, SPF, DKIM, DMARC) and each one can break independently.</p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Diagnostic checklist</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li class="leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-ink">Incoming mail broken?</strong> Check MX records with the record lookup. Were they accidentally deleted or changed during the DNS update?</li><li class="leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-ink">Outgoing mail going to spam?</strong> Check SPF (was it modified?), DKIM (is the selector still resolving?), and DMARC (is the policy still published?).</li><li class="leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-ink">Mail rejected with 5.7.x?</strong> A policy rejection: check the <a href="/blacklist-checker/">blacklist checker</a> and verify authentication records.</li><li class="leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-ink">Everything broken at once?</strong> The nameserver change may have replaced the entire zone. Check whether MX, SPF, and DKIM records still exist.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">The recovery procedure</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li class="leading-relaxed">Identify which records are missing or changed (record lookup for each type).</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Restore them from your backup (you exported before the change, right?).</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Verify each record type individually: MX, SPF, DKIM, DMARC.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Watch the propagation checker until all resolvers agree.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Send test messages to Gmail, Outlook, and a colleague to confirm delivery.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why does email break more often than websites after DNS changes?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Websites need one record (A). Email needs four working together (MX, SPF, DKIM, DMARC): four times the chances to break.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I receive mail if MX records are missing?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Not to your domain: senders have no routing instruction. Some servers fall back to the A record, but this is unreliable.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How long until email works after fixing MX?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The DNS fix propagates in minutes to hours. In-flight mail that bounced is lost (sender gets a bounce notice); new mail delivers normally.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Should I test email after DNS changes?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes: send to yourself at Gmail, Outlook, and a colleague. Check both delivery and spam placement.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What if only external senders can't reach me?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Internal mail may work via direct delivery. The issue is your MX records as seen by external resolvers: check propagation.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does changing my A record affect email?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Only indirectly: if your mail server runs on the same IP as your website, changing the A record redirects web traffic but not mail (MX points at a hostname, not an IP).</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/mx-record/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">MX records →</a></li><li><a href="/spf-record/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF records →</a></li><li><a href="/dmarc/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC guide →</a></li></ul>

    <div class="mt-10 flex items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 px-5 py-3">
      <span class="grid h-9 w-9 place-items-center rounded-full bg-gradient-to-br from-accent to-violet font-display text-xs font-bold text-canvas">PD</span>
      <div>
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">PromptDNS Team</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-ink-faint">Last updated: August 2026</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
